Subject: moving desktop icons, a see-through copy of the icon follows the pointer

On Windows, when you move an icon on the desktop, the icon stays at its
place, dimmed, and a see-through copy of it and its name follows the
pointer. Several icons travel as a small stack with their number on it.
Here the icon vanished from its place for the whole drag: FolderModel
reports dragged rows as "blank", and the delegate unloads a blank row.
And the picture under the pointer was a grab of the whole delegate, with
the selection box and the round "open" button drawn on hover.

Now:

- dragged rows get a "dragged" role of their own. They stay blank for
  everything that decides where things go (drops, the positioner, folder
  hover), so nothing about moving changes; only the delegate reads the
  new role, keeps its icon and name loaded and draws them at 45 %.
  FolderView marks the icons as landed the moment the positioner moves
  them, so a dropped icon is at full strength at once, not dimmed until
  the drag has wound up.
- the delegate grabs its icon and its label separately and hands them
  over with their rectangles. rayedragghost.cpp draws the copy: icon and
  name at 70 %, or for several icons the pressed one on top of up to two
  others, with a count in the accent colour. The stack is drawn whole and
  faded once, so the top icon covers the ones behind it.
- each icon's cursor offset comes from its cell again, as upstream, not
  from the picture: where icons land does not depend on how the copy is
  drawn, and a group keeps its shape.

Reason: this cannot be done outside the source. The picture is taken by
the containment's own QML package and composed in FolderModel before the
drag starts, and the delegate unloads a dragged row because of a role
the model computes; no setting, script or look-and-feel package reaches
any of it.

